    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` behavior for `POST /api/owners`. When the request sets `sharesHousehold: true` and another owner already lives at the same address under the same last name, the new owner is allowed (rather than rejected as a duplicate household), and both the joining owner and every existing household member are stamped with the same stable `householdId`, which is now returned on the owner.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n- **`Household.java`** (new helper) \u2014 the single definition of a household: `matches(owner, lastName, address)` for the case-insensitive/whitespace-normalized membership test, and `id(lastName, address)` for the stable shared identifier (a deterministic name-based UUID derived from the normalized last name + address, so every member resolves to the same value regardless of creation order).\n\n- **`EnsureUniqueHousehold.java`** \u2014 refactored to reuse `Household.matches` instead of its own inlined normalization/matching, keeping its single responsibility (reject duplicate households unless opted in).\n\n- **`AssignHousehold.java`** (new pipeline step) \u2014 the opt-in path: when `sharesHousehold` is true and the new owner joins an existing household, assigns the shared `householdId` to the new owner and every existing member (saving them). No-op otherwise.\n\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `assignHousehold` between `build` and `defaultRegistrationDate`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the persisted `householdId` field with accessors (mirroring `customerCode`).\n\n- **openapi.yml** \u2014 added `householdId` as a read-only property on the `Owner` schema, so it flows into `OwnerDto` and is auto-mapped by MapStruct.\n\n- **schema.sql** (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql) \u2014 added the `household_id` column.\n\nThe existing duplicate-household rejection (409 when not opting in) is unchanged, and `sharesHousehold` remains write-only. The full test suite is green.",
